('m-a1-set-a-16', 3, '3.5.3', 'A', 'essay', 25, 'Answer EITHER (a) OR (b). Start your answer by writing (a) or (b).

(a) Since 2016 the UK National Living Wage has generally risen faster than average earnings, and by 2024 it had reached a target of around two-thirds of median hourly earnings. Evaluate the likely effects of large increases in the National Living Wage on workers and firms in low-pay sectors such as hospitality and adult social care.

(b) In 2018 Scotland introduced a minimum price per unit of alcohol, and the minimum price was raised in 2024. Evaluate whether minimum prices are the most effective way for governments to reduce the consumption of demerit goods such as alcohol.', null, null, 'mock'),
('m-a1-set-b-01', 1, '1.2.10', 'A', 'mcq', 1, 'A restaurant places a £120 bottle of wine at the top of its wine list, although it rarely sells one. Sales of the £40 bottles listed beneath it increase. Which concept best explains the change in customer behaviour?', null, '["A Altruism","B Rational choice based on perfect information","C Anchoring","D Habitual behaviour"]'::jsonb, 'mock'),
('m-a1-set-b-02', 1, '1.2.1', 'A', 'mcq', 1, 'A consumer spends all of her weekly budget on coffees costing £3 each and sandwiches costing £5 each. For the last coffee she buys, her marginal utility is 30 units; for the last sandwich it is 40 units. To maximise her total utility with the same budget she should', null, '["A buy more sandwiches and fewer coffees.","B buy more coffees and fewer sandwiches.","C make no change, because she is already maximising utility.","D buy fewer of both goods."]'::jsonb, 'mock'),
('m-a1-set-b-03', 3, '3.3.1', 'A', 'mcq', 1, 'A firm faces a straight-line, downward-sloping demand (average revenue) curve. At the output at which its marginal revenue is zero,', null, '["A total revenue is maximised and price elasticity of demand is equal to −1.","B profit is maximised.","C demand is price elastic, so a price cut would raise total revenue.","D average revenue is also zero."]'::jsonb, 'mock'),
('m-a1-set-b-04', 3, '3.4.6', 'A', 'mcq', 1, 'A hospital trust is the only major employer of nurses in a town. It employs 10 nurses at £600 per week each. To recruit an eleventh nurse it must raise the weekly wage to £620 for all its nurses. What is the marginal cost of labour of the eleventh nurse?', null, '["A £20","B £620","C £6,820","D £820"]'::jsonb, 'mock'),
('m-a1-set-b-05', 3, '3.5.2', 'A', 'mcq', 1, 'Which of the following would most likely cause a rightward shift of the supply curve of labour to the nursing profession?', null, '["A A rise in the wage rate paid to nurses","B A fall in the demand for hospital treatment","C An improvement in working conditions, including more flexible shift patterns","D An increase in the length of training needed to qualify as a nurse"]'::jsonb, 'mock'),
