('m-a3-set-a-08', 4, '4.5.4', 'A', 'essay', 25, 'Answer EITHER (a) OR (b). Start your answer by writing (a) or (b).

(a) Using the information provided and your own knowledge, evaluate whether a universal Energy Price Guarantee, as described in Extract B, was the best policy response by Norland''s government to the energy price shock.

(b) Using the information provided and your own knowledge, evaluate the view that Norland''s central bank was right to raise interest rates sharply in response to inflation caused by a rise in the price of imported energy.', 'Norland: an energy price shock (illustrative data)

Norland is a fictional high-income economy with a floating exchange rate and an independent central bank with a 2% inflation target. It imports most of the natural gas it uses for heating and electricity generation. Its currency is the Norland dollar (N$).

Table 1: Selected economic indicators for Norland, 2021-2025 (illustrative data)
Year | Wholesale gas price index (2021 = 100) | CPI inflation (%) | Central bank policy rate (%, end of year) | Real GDP growth (%) | Unemployment rate (%) | Current account balance (% of GDP)
2021 | 100 | 2.5 | 0.25 | 6.0 | 4.6 | -1.5
2022 | 290 | 9.0 | 3.00 | 3.8 | 3.9 | -4.0
2023 | 170 | 7.0 | 5.25 | 0.3 | 4.1 | -3.2
2024 | 130 | 2.8 | 4.75 | 0.9 | 4.4 | -2.5
2025 | 125 | 3.2 | 4.00 | 1.1 | 4.8 | -2.2

Table 2: Norland''s household energy retail market (illustrative data)
Indicator | 2021 | 2025
Number of household energy suppliers | 48 | 22
Combined market share of the five largest suppliers (%) | 70 | 86
Customers switching supplier each month (% of all customers) | 1.8 | 0.4

Extract A: From shock to squeeze
When wholesale gas prices nearly tripled in 2022 after supply disruption abroad, Norland''s import bill jumped and inflation rose to its highest rate in three decades. Firms in energy-intensive industries such as chemicals, glass and ceramics cut output, and some postponed investment. Nominal wages rose by around 6% in 2023, but households'' real disposable income fell. The central bank raised its policy rate repeatedly between late 2021 and 2023, arguing that it needed to stop high inflation becoming embedded in wage settlements and expectations. Critics argued that higher interest rates could do nothing about the world price of gas and would only deepen the slowdown.

Extract B: The Energy Price Guarantee
In late 2022 the government capped the unit price that suppliers could charge households and paid suppliers the difference between the capped price and their cost of supplying energy. The guarantee applied to all households regardless of income and cost around 2% of GDP in its first year. It was funded by extra borrowing, and the budget deficit rose to 6% of GDP. The finance minister said the guarantee ''took around three percentage points off peak inflation''. Some economists argued that a scheme targeted at low-income households and those with high energy needs, paid for partly by a windfall tax on energy producers'' profits, would have been cheaper and would have kept a stronger incentive for better-off households to cut energy use.

Extract C: Supplier failures
Before 2021, dozens of small suppliers had entered Norland''s household energy market, attracted by low wholesale prices and a regulated price cap that left room for profit. Many sold fixed-price deals without buying energy in advance to cover them. When wholesale prices rose above the level allowed by the cap, they made a loss on every customer and 26 suppliers collapsed. Their customers were transferred to larger suppliers, and the cost of around N$2.5 billion was added to all households'' bills. The regulator has since introduced tougher capital and hedging requirements for new and existing suppliers. The largest firms say these rules make the market more stable; consumer groups say the market has become less competitive and that customer service has worsened.', null, 'mock'),
('m-a3-set-b-01', 3, '3.3.2', 'A', 'data', 5, 'Using Table 1, calculate the percentage increase in the total annual cost to an employer of a full-time worker paid the National Living Wage between 2024-25 and 2025-26. Show your workings.', 'Rising costs in UK hospitality

Table 1: Annual cost to an employer of a full-time hospitality worker aged 21 or over paid the National Living Wage (NLW rates and employer National Insurance rules as they applied in each year; hours are illustrative)
Item | 2024-25 | 2025-26
National Living Wage (£ per hour) | 11.44 | 12.21
Hours worked per year | 1,560 | 1,560
Employer National Insurance contribution (NIC) rate (%) | 13.8 | 15.0
Annual earnings threshold above which employer NICs are paid (£) | 9,100 | 5,000
Note: ignore pension contributions and the Employment Allowance.

Table 2: Harbour Street Taverns, a fictional pub and restaurant group, 2023-2025 (illustrative data)
Year | Revenue (£m) | Staff costs (£m) | Energy and food costs (£m) | Operating profit (£m) | Number of sites | Employees
2023 | 210 | 66 | 71 | 18.0 | 140 | 5,600
2024 | 222 | 72 | 74 | 15.5 | 138 | 5,450
2025 | 230 | 80 | 76 | 9.8 | 129 | 5,050

Extract A: A tough year for pubs and restaurants
Hospitality is one of the UK''s largest employers, with a high proportion of part-time, young and low-paid workers. The sector is made up of thousands of independent cafés, pubs and restaurants alongside a smaller number of chains such as Harbour Street Taverns. Consumers can choose between many outlets in most towns, and businesses compete through menus, atmosphere and location as much as through price. From April 2025 the National Living Wage rose by 6.7% and employers'' National Insurance contributions increased. Industry surveys during 2025 reported that many operators had cut staff hours, frozen recruitment or raised menu prices, and a number of independent venues closed. Harbour Street Taverns has closed some of its least profitable sites and plans to introduce self-service ordering tablets in 60 of its pubs.

Extract B: Raising revenue
The government said the increase in employer NICs was needed to raise revenue for public services, including the NHS, without raising the main rates of income tax, employee National Insurance or VAT. At the same time it increased the Employment Allowance, which reduces the employer NICs bill of eligible employers, so that many of the smallest businesses pay less than before. Many economists expect that, over time, much of the cost of higher employer NICs will be passed on to workers through slower growth in pay than would otherwise have occurred, with some passed on to consumers through higher prices and some absorbed by firms through lower profits. The government''s target is for the NLW to be at least two-thirds of median hourly earnings for workers aged 21 and over.

Extract C: Shortages, turnover and productivity
Despite job cuts in some areas, many hospitality employers in tourist areas still report difficulty recruiting chefs and front-of-house staff. Changes to immigration rules since 2021 have reduced the supply of EU workers to the sector, and staff turnover is high. Output per hour in hospitality is among the lowest of any UK sector. Supporters of higher minimum wages argue that they push firms to invest in training and technology, reduce costly staff turnover and raise the living standards of low-paid households. Critics argue that young and less-skilled workers will find it harder to get their first job, and that higher costs will be passed on as higher prices across the economy.', null, 'mock'),
