Around 400,000 people work in Veldonia''s car industry, mostly in two northern regions where unemployment is above the national average. Domestic carmakers say they need time to reach the scale of their Asterland rivals. Consumer groups argue that the tariff will raise prices for households and slow the switch to EVs, at a time when Veldonia''s inflation rate is 3.4%, above the central bank''s 2% target. Some economists argue that the government should instead adopt an industrial strategy: subsidising battery ''gigafactories'', funding training in battery engineering, investing in public charging points and speeding up planning decisions, while keeping its markets open to imports. Others warn that governments are poor at ''picking winners'' and that subsidy races between countries waste taxpayers'' money.', null, 'mock'),
('m-as1-set-a-01', 1, '1.1.1', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'Economic theory predicts that, ceteris paribus, an increase in bus fares will reduce the number of bus journeys made. In one year a town''s bus fares rose by 5%, yet the number of bus journeys increased. Which of the following is the most likely explanation that is consistent with the theory?', null, '["A Demand for bus travel in the town is price inelastic.","B The prediction is a normative statement, so it cannot be tested against evidence.","C Petrol prices and town-centre parking charges rose sharply in the same year.","D Bus operators earned more total revenue after the fare rise."]'::jsonb, 'mock'),
('m-as1-set-a-02', 1, '1.1.4', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'An economy is currently producing at a point inside its production possibility frontier (PPF). Which of the following would move the economy towards its existing frontier without shifting the frontier?', null, '["A A fall in cyclical unemployment","B Net inward migration of working-age people","C The discovery of new reserves of natural gas","D An improvement in the quality of education and training"]'::jsonb, 'mock'),
('m-as1-set-a-03', 1, '1.2.8', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'The demand curve for tickets to a music festival is a straight line. At a price of £60 no tickets would be demanded. At a price of £20, 4,000 tickets are sold. The organiser raises the price to £30 and sells 3,000 tickets. By how much does consumer surplus fall?', null, '["A £10,000","B £35,000","C £45,000","D £80,000"]'::jsonb, 'mock'),
