The Brenland Farmers'' Union is campaigning for a guaranteed minimum farm-gate price of 38p per litre. Under its plan, a government agency would buy at 38p any milk that processors did not want, and turn it into butter and milk powder to be stored and sold later. The Union estimates that at 38p, production would be around 3% higher than in 2024 within two years, while purchases by processors would be around 1% lower. Treasury officials point out that when the European Economic Community guaranteed high prices for dairy products in the 1970s and 1980s, it built up large stores of surplus butter and milk powder, and eventually introduced milk production quotas in 1984. Supermarkets, which often sell milk cheaply to attract shoppers, warn that the plan would raise shelf prices. Alternative proposals include payments to farmers for environmental improvements such as planting hedgerows, help for farmers to agree fixed-price contracts with processors in advance, and an investigation by the competition authority into whether supermarkets and processors use their buying power to hold down farm-gate prices. Some economists argue that the government should not intervene at all, since the exit of high-cost farms allows resources to move to more efficient producers.', null, 'mock'),
